1. `index.js`

	•  Purpose: This is the entry point of the React application. It renders the root component (App.js) into the DOM.
	•  Sequence: It initializes the application and sets up the React environment.
	•  Example: When the application starts, index.js renders the App component, which contains the routing logic.
	
	
	
2. `App.js`

	•  Purpose: This file defines the routing for the application using react-router-dom. It determines which component (Home or Details) to render based on the URL.
	•  Sequence: After index.js, App.js renders the appropriate component based on the route.
	•  Example:
		•  When the user visits /, the Home component is rendered.
		•  When the user navigates to /details/:id, the Details component is rendered.
		
		
		
3. `Home.js`

	•  Purpose: This file displays a list of options fetched from the backend and allows the user to select one. It also provides navigation to the Details page.
	•  Sequence: When the user visits /, Home.js is rendered. It fetches the options and displays them in a dropdown.
	•  Example:
		•  The user selects an option and clicks "Go," which navigates to /details/:id (e.g., /details/1).
		
		
4. `Details.js`

	•  Purpose: This file fetches the details of the selected option and allows the user to update it. 
	It also sends the updated value to the backend.

Example
When the user navigates to /details/:id, Details.js is rendered. It fetches the details of the selected option and displays an input field for editing.
•  When The user edits the value and clicks "Update." A POST request is sent to the backend to update the option. After a successful update, the user is redirected back to / (the Home page).
Example Flow for Updating Options:

	1. User visits `/`:
	2. index.js renders App.js.
	3. App.js renders Home.js.
	4. Home.js fetches and displays the list of options.
	5. User selects an option and clicks "Go":
	6. Home.js navigates to /details/:id (e.g., /details/1).
	7. User edits the option on `/details/:id`:
	8. Details.js fetches the details of the selected option.
	9. The user updates the value and clicks "Update."
	10. A POST request is sent to the backend to update the option.
	11. After the update, the user is redirected back to /.
	12. User sees the updated list on `/`:
	13. Home.js fetches the updated list of options from the backend.